course: biology
lesson: 341
title: Where milk comes from: cow to carton, cheese, butter and yoghurt
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Milk is baby food. A cow makes it only after she has had a calf, just as a mother cat or a mother pig makes milk for her own young, and dairy cows are bred to make far more than one calf can drink.
explain: Look under a cow at a dairy farm and you can count four teats, one for each quarter of the udder, which is why a milking machine has four cups. Many cows now walk into a milking robot all by themselves, because it means a tasty snack and a lighter udder.
explain: From the farm the milk travels cold in a tanker to the dairy, where a quick heating called pasteurisation kills the germs. Then it goes into cartons and bottles and back into the cold, all the way to your fridge.
explain: Milk can also be changed. Shake cream in a jar and the fat clumps into butter; thicken milk into curds and press them and you get cheese; let friendly bacteria grow in warm milk and it turns into yoghurt.
explain: And forget the cartoon cheese full of round holes: only a few cheeses, like Swiss Emmental, have holes, blown by gas from tiny bacteria.
fact: A cow's udder has four quarters, each with its own milk gland and its own teat; a goat or a sheep has two teats and a pig about fourteen.
fact: A cow gives milk only after she has had a calf; dairy cows usually calve about once a year and give milk for about ten months after, and a good one gives about 25 to 30 litres a day.
fact: In a milking robot, cameras and lasers find the four teats and a robot arm fixes the cups on while the cow eats a treat, and some cows choose to be milked three times a day.
fact: Milk is cooled to about 4 degrees straight after milking, and at the dairy it is heated to about 72 degrees for about 15 seconds; this pasteurisation, named after Louis Pasteur, kills germs.
fact: Pasteurised milk keeps about a week in the fridge, while UHT milk, heated much hotter for a few seconds, keeps for months on a shelf until it is opened.
fact: Milk fat floats in tiny drops and rises as cream; most shop milk is homogenised, squeezed through tiny holes so the drops are too small to rise.
fact: In Britain blue caps usually mean whole milk, green semi-skimmed and red skimmed, which has had most of its fat taken out.
fact: Shaking or churning cream makes the fat drops stick together into butter, leaving buttermilk, and about 20 litres of milk make one kilogram of butter.
fact: Cheese is made from curds, the soft lumps of thickened milk, pressed and ripened, while whey is the watery liquid left; cheesemakers add bacteria and rennet, a helper that makes milk set into curds; most of the milk sugar drains away with the whey, and about ten litres of milk make a kilogram of hard cheese.
fact: Only some cheeses, like Emmental, have holes; they are gas bubbles made by bacteria, and Swiss scientists reported that specks of hay dust in the milk help them start.
fact: Yoghurt is milk changed by friendly bacteria kept warm at about 40 to 45 degrees; they eat the milk sugar and make acid, which thickens the milk and makes it sharp.
fact: People also milk goats, sheep, water buffalo, camels, yaks, horses and reindeer; feta is usually sheep's milk and real mozzarella is buffalo milk.
fact: Many adults lose most of lactase, the body chemical that breaks down milk sugar, so milk gives them gas and a sore tummy; this happens in about two thirds of the world's adults but much less in northern Europe.
exercise: How many teats does a cow's udder have? | Four | Two | Six | Eight
exercise: Why does a milking machine have four cups? | Each quarter of the udder has its own teat | Four cups milk twice as fast as two would on the same cow | One cup is spare in case another one breaks | Each cup collects a different kind of milk
exercise: When does a cow start to make milk? | After she has had a calf | As soon as she starts eating fresh grass | Only when she is milked by a robot | From the day she is born
exercise: About how much milk does a good dairy cow give in a day? | About 25 to 30 litres | About one cupful | About 2 litres | About 300 litres
exercise: Why do cows walk into a milking robot on their own? | It gives them a treat and a lighter, more comfortable udder | The farmer pushes them in from behind with a tractor every time | They think the robot is their own calf | They are frightened of the field
exercise: How does a milking robot find the teats? | With cameras and lasers | By sniffing for the smell of the milk | The cow points to them with her hoof | It guesses until it gets it right
exercise: What does pasteurisation do to milk? | A quick heating kills the germs | It adds extra cream to the milk | It freezes the milk solid for weeks | It turns the milk into soft cheese
exercise: Why is UHT milk able to stay on a shelf for months? | It was heated much hotter for a few seconds | It has lots of sugar added to keep it sweet | It comes from a special breed of long-life cow | It has all the water taken out
exercise: Why does cream rise to the top of an old-fashioned milk bottle? | Fat drops are lighter than water and float up | The cream is heavier and gets pushed up by the milk | The bottle was shaken on the doorstep | Cream is made from the bottle's foil cap
exercise: What does homogenised milk mean? | The fat drops were made too small to rise | All the fat was taken out of the milk at the dairy | The milk came from one single cow only | The milk was mixed with lots of water
exercise: What happens when you shake cream in a jar for a long time? | The fat drops stick together into butter | The cream slowly turns into thick yoghurt | The cream turns into a cheese full of holes | Nothing, it only gets warm
exercise: In the rhyme, Little Miss Muffet eats curds and whey. What are curds? | The soft lumps of thickened milk that cheese is made from | The thin, watery yellow liquid left over after cheese is made | The cream skimmed off the top of the milk | The butter from a churn
exercise: Where do the holes in Swiss Emmental come from? | Bubbles of gas made by bacteria while it ripens | Mice nibbling tunnels in the cheese down in the cellar | Cheesemakers drilling them with a tool | Air blown in through a straw
exercise: What turns warm milk into yoghurt? | Friendly bacteria that eat milk sugar and make acid | Adding a big spoon of lemon juice and a cup of white flour | Freezing it hard and thawing it out again | Leaving it in the sun for a week
exercise: Real mozzarella is made from the milk of which animal? | Water buffalo | Camel | Goat | Reindeer
exercise: Why does milk give some adults a sore tummy? | They have lost most of the chemical that breaks down milk sugar | Milk slowly turns into a poison inside the body of every single grown-up | They always drink it far too cold | Their stomach has grown too small
test: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry nibbles a wedge of yellow cheese full of round holes. What is wrong with the cartoon? | Most cheeses, like cheddar, have no holes; only a few like Emmental do | Nothing is wrong, because every kind of cheese has holes right through it | Cheese holes are made by mice eating it | Cheese with holes is not real cheese
test: «mate says bulls give milk too, just less of it» Is he right? | No, only a cow who has had a calf makes milk | Yes, bulls give about half as much milk as a cow does | Yes, but only in winter | Yes, but bull milk is blue
test: Count them: 3 cows stand in the milking shed. How many teats does the machine need cups for, and how many tails swish? | 12 teats and 3 tails | 6 teats and 3 tails | 12 teats and 6 tails | 8 teats and 3 tails
test: «a goat's got four teats like a cow, right?» What is the best answer? | No, a goat has two teats | Yes, exactly four | No, a goat has fourteen | No, a goat has only one
test: What would happen to the milk supply if every cow on a farm had her calf in the same week? | There would be loads of milk for months, then almost none at once | The milk would stay exactly the same all year round, week after week after week | The cows would give no milk at all | The milk would turn into cream
test: «leave the milk out, it's fine, it's from the shop innit» Why should the milk go back in the fridge? | Germs that make milk go bad grow fast when it is warm | Milk freezes solid if it is left out on the table | Shop milk has no germs ever, so it does not matter where it goes | Cold milk has more fat in it
test: «it's taken bloody ages, still just cream sloshing about» What will appear if he keeps shaking the jar? | A lump of butter and some thin buttermilk | A big block of hard yellow cheese with holes in it | A pot of thick, sour yoghurt | Ice cream
test: Why does it take so many litres of milk to make one kilogram of butter or cheese? | Milk is mostly water, and only a small part becomes butter or cheese | The cows drink most of the milk back | Half the milk is spilt on the way | Butter and cheese need milk to be boiled away completely
test: You live high in the Himalayas where cows struggle. Which animal would people there most likely milk? | A yak | A water buffalo | A camel | A reindeer
test: «my friend gets tummy ache from milk but eats cheddar fine, she's faking» What is the best reply? | Hard cheese and yoghurt have much less milk sugar left, so they can upset her less | She must be faking, because cheese is just milk and should hurt her tummy the same | Cheddar has extra milk sugar in it | Cheese is made from plants, not milk
test: In the film Barnyard, Otis, a male cow (a bull), has a big pink udder. What would a farmer say? | Bulls have no udders; only cows have udders and make milk | Right, every bull has an udder and gives milk, just like every cow does | Right, but bull udders are smaller | Only bulls have udders, cows do not
test: A sign on a carton says «UHT». What can you say about the milk before it is opened? | It can keep for months on a shelf | It must be drunk within a day | It is milk from a camel | It has had all its water taken out
exam: Identify the condition under which a cow commences the production of milk. | Subsequent to the birth of a calf | Upon the consumption of a sufficient quantity of grass | Exclusively when milked by mechanical apparatus | Continuously from the moment of her own birth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process of pasteurisation? | The milk is heated to approximately seventy-two degrees for fifteen seconds so that germs are destroyed | The milk is frozen to a very low temperature for several days so that all germs are rendered inactive permanently | The fat of the milk is removed so that it keeps indefinitely | The milk is mixed with acid so that it thickens
exam: Ascertain the origin of the cavities observed in cheeses of the Emmental type. | Gas produced by bacteria during the ripening of the cheese | The consumption of the cheese by rodents during storage | The deliberate perforation of the cheese by its maker | The evaporation of whey from the surface of the cheese
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the production of yoghurt. | Bacteria consume the sugar of milk kept at about forty degrees and produce acid that thickens it | Milk is boiled for several hours until all of its water is removed and the solid residue sets firmly in the pot | Milk is frozen and subsequently thawed so that it thickens | Flour and sugar are added to cold milk and stirred
